  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings and predictive simulations are based on the specific environmental and urban conditions of Beijing, meaning the identified relationships and optimization potentials may differ in other geographic regions.",
      "what": "This study combined GEDI spaceborne LiDAR, terrestrial laser scanning, and machine learning models to analyze the relationship between canopy structural complexity (CSC) and net primary productivity in Beijing's urban forests. The researchers found that higher CSC correlates with increased productivity, with natural forests showing stronger CSC-carbon sequestration associations than managed ones. Scenario-based simulations demonstrated that optimizing canopy structure could boost overall forest productivity by up to 21.2%, and by up to 47.8% in specific managed forest areas.",
      "why": "It establishes three-dimensional canopy structure as a measurable, actionable indicator for urban forest management, demonstrating that structural optimization can significantly increase carbon sequestration without expanding forest area."
    }
